                "senderName": "NWS Salt Lake City UT",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 11 at 10:35PM MDT until October 12 at 6:00PM MDT by NWS Salt Lake City UT",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of Utah, including the following areas, Castle\nCountry, Central Mountains, Eastern Juab/Millard Counties, San\nRafael Swell, Sanpete Valley, Sevier Valley, Wasatch Plateau/Book\nCliffs, Bryce Canyon Country, Capitol Reef National Park and\nVicinity, Glen Canyon Recreation Area/Lake Powell, South Central\nUtah, Southern Mountains, Upper Sevier River Valleys, Western\nCanyonlands, Lower Washington County, Southwest Utah and Zion\nNational Park. This also includes recent burn scars such as\nForsyth, Cottonwood, Silver King, France Canyon, and Monroe Canyon.\n\n* WHEN...Through Monday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, dry washes, slot canyons, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations such as slickrock areas and recent burn\nscars. Creeks and streams may rise out of their banks. Flooding may\noccur in poor drainage and urban areas.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Anomalously high moisture from the remnants of Tropical Storm\nRachel will continue to impact the region. While this is expected\nto be a long duration rainfall event, scattered instances of\nheavier rainfall rates are expected with stronger storms yielding\nflash flooding concerns given saturated soils and prior burn\nscars.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
