                "description": "* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of Utah, including the following areas, Western\nMillard and Juab Counties, Bear Lake and Bear River Valley, Cache\nValley/Utah Portion, Eastern Box Elder County, Great Salt Lake\nDesert and Mountains, Northern Wasatch Front, Salt Lake Valley,\nTooele and Rush Valleys, Utah Valley, Wasatch Back, Wasatch\nMountains I-80 North, Wasatch Mountains South of I-80, Western\nUinta Basin and Western Uinta Mountains. This includes recent burn\nscars such as Rocky Canyon.\n\n* WHEN...Through Monday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and recent burn scars. Flooding may occur in poor\ndrainage and urban areas. Extensive street flooding and flooding of\ncreeks and rivers are possible.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Moisture associated with the remnants of Tropical Storm Rachel\nwill continue to spread into northern Utah producing a prolonged\nrainfall event. In particular, moisture interacting with a\nfrontal boundary across far northern Utah may yield locally\nheavier rainfall totals compared to other locations. As a result,\nareal flooding is possible across northern Utah.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
